fact WorkItem(id: "WI-639", description: some(value: "Distributive dot projection `x.(m1, ..., mn)`: a general syntactic form generalizing WI-638's single-field dot access `x.f` to a MEMBER LIST. `x.(m1, ..., mn)` desugars to the ORDERED/NAMED tuple `(m1: x.m1, ..., mn: x.mn)` -- distribute the receiver x over the member list; each `x.mi` is ordinary dot-dispatch (a field via WI-638, but any member/method/component), and the result is keyed by the member names.\n\nDESUGARING: a bare member auto-labels (the member name is BOTH the result key AND the dot-member): `x.(f1, f2)` => `(f1: x.f1, f2: x.f2)`. Rename via colon: `x.(a: f1, b: f2)` => `(a: x.f1, b: x.f2)`. A single member 1-collapses: `x.(f)` => `(f: x.f)` -> collapses to `x.f`.\n\nTWO LOAD-BEARING RULES: (1) the result is the ORDERED/NAMED tuple, NOT positional -- labels are preserved from the member names, never auto-named _1/_2 (a positional result would destroy a projected relation's schema). (2) members resolve at TYPING, not at name-resolution -- each mi lands in `field_access(x, mi)` dot-position, resolved against x's type at the typer (WI-638's resolve_field_type, dot-dispatch mode-3), riding UNRESOLVED past the name resolver; a member name is therefore NEVER a value-position scope symbol, so BOTH keep (`x.(f1, f2)`) and rename (`x.(a: f1)`) resolve safely with no free-identifier hazard (this is precisely why a bare `select(x, y)` surface was rejected in proposal 052).\n\nTOUCH-POINTS (mirror WI-638): (a) grammar (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js) -- a new `x.(...)` form: dot + a parenthesized member/rename list (`.(` is currently free; field access today is `x.ident`). GOTCHA: run `cargo clean -p tree-sitter-anthill` after any grammar.js change or the build uses a stale parser. (b) parse/convert.rs -- desugar `x.(m1, ..., mn)` -> named-tuple literal `(m1: field_access(x, m1), ...)`, duplicating a bare member into both the label and the field_access member; a rename `a: f1` -> label `a` + `field_access(x, f1)`. Desugaring EARLY (at convert) means the existing WI-638 field-access typing + named-tuple-literal typing + eval handle the rest with no new typer/eval arm. (c) typer/eval -- reuse WI-638 dot-dispatch (typing.rs resolve_field_type NamedTuple arm) + named-tuple construction; verify auto-label and 1-collapse.\n\nGENERAL FEATURE: works on ANY named tuple / any receiver with dot-members, not just relations -- useful to any tuple consumer, exactly as WI-638 was.\n\nDRIVER: proposal 052 (docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md, section \"Projection -- the distribute-dot\"). 052 lifts this over a relation -- `r.(f1, f2)` -> `projected` (schema-preserving via the name-keying) -- RETIRING the `select` op; 052 depends on this the way it now depends on WI-638, but the feature is general and 052 merely consumes it.\n\nOPEN SUB-DECISIONS (052 OQ3): a non-bare member that is a call or expression (`x.(count(), y)`) needs a label rule (label = the head identifier, or an explicit `n: expr`); a positional variant is deferred (default: named only -- write `(x.f1, x.f2)` explicitly for positional); an exclude form (`x.(-f3)`) is deferred (the Mongo `(f3: 0)` variant's one edge, rejected for its magic flags).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `(x: 10, y: 20, z: 30).(x, y)` evaluates to `(x: 10, y: 20)`; rename `(x: 10, y: 20).(a: x, b: y)` -> `(a: 10, b: 20)`; single `(x: 10, y: 20).(x)` 1-collapses to 10; a param typed `t: (x: Int64, y: Int64)` -- `t.(x, y)` typechecks and evaluates; members resolve at typing (no value-position name-resolution of a member); existing single-field `x.f` (WI-638) and positional `t._N` are unaffected; cargo test green with a test paralleling tests/include/wi638_named_tuple_field_test.rs. REF: WI-638 (named-tuple field access, delivered -- the single-field basis + resolve_field_type / dot-dispatch mode-3), kernel-language.md 6.7 (dot projection -- extend with a member-list form; currently three modes), 4.6 (named tuples are ordered products). Rust-only (typer/parser; scaland has no typer)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-638"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T13:08:45Z"))
